Zirconium-based $\mathrm{MOFs}^{28,36}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{Zr}\mathrm{-}M O F s})$ represent a remarkable MOF subclass on account of the strong $z\mathrm{r-O}$ coordination bond, and they have received considerable attention in the field since the first $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFi.e., UiO-66 (where $^{\omega}\mathrm{UiO}^{\prime\prime}$ denotes “University of Oslo”) with $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ inorganic building blocks and the 12-connected (12-c) fcu topologywas reported.3 Tremendous efforts have been devoted to the development of Zr-MOFs based on edge-transitive netsi.e., nets with one type of edge because of the upsurge in reticular chemistry and versatile connectivity of polynuclear $Z\mathrm{r}$ clusters.28 Many applications of these $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs have been investigated, from heterogeneous catalysis4,8 to water capture.31,32 These edge-transitive networks include but are not limited to spn (e.g., MOF-808;31 triangle and trigonal antiprism), she (e.g., PCN-224;33 square and hexagon), hxg (e.g., pbz-MOF-1;34 hexagon), the $(\mathrm{e.g.,NU-}1200^{35}/$ BUT12;36 triangle and cube), csq $(\mathrm{e.g.,PCN{-}}222^{37}/\mathrm{MOF{-}}545^{38}$ and NU-1000;39 square and cube), flu (e.g., PCN-521;22 tetrahedron and cube), reo (e.g., DUT-51;40 cube); ftw (e.g., MOF$525^{38}$ and MOF-1211;41 square and cuboctahedron), ith (e.g., MOF-812;31 tetrahedron and icosahedron), and shp (e.g., PCN-223; square and hexagonal prism) (see Table S2 for a complete list). Nevertheless, an important class of ${\bf{Z r{-}M O F s}}$ based on a highly connected edge-transitive net, the 6,12-c alb net,21,24,43 (trigonal prism and hexagonal prism; “alb” denotes “aluminum diboride”), has yet to be reported, most likely because of the lack of synthetic accessibility of the rigid trigonalprismatic ligands and suitable assembly conditions. Higher connectivity in MOF structures permits an increased degree of controllability and designability. It is important to note that the synthesis of alb-MOFs based on rare-earth polynuclear clusters and indium trimers employing reticular chemistry has been reported recently by the Eddaoudi group.24,43
Herein we report the reticular synthesis of a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF with the rare $\scriptstyle6,12-c$ alb net, NU-1600, constructed from threefolddisordered $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes42,44 and the peripherally extended triptycene $\mathrm{'H}_{6}\mathrm{PET-1}\rangle$ ligands25,45 with a rigid trigonal-prismatic shape. Single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (SCXRD) analysis revealed that the 6-c trigonal-prismatic PET-1 ligands and the $12\mathrm{-c}\ Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ nodes act as rare hexagonal-prismatic secondary building units (SBUs), leading to the assembly of 3-periodic NU-1600 with the alb net (Figure 1). NU-1600 is microporous, with an apparent Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $2085\ \hat{\mathrm{m}^{2}}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and an experimental pore volume (PV) of 0.82 $\mathsf{c m}^{3}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , as revealed by its $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at $77\mathrm{K}.$ The expanded mesoporous isoreticular structuresi.e., NU-1601 and NU-1602, with experimental PVs of up to $2.36~\mathsf{c m}^{3}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}-$ can be readily synthesized (Figure S1) with extended ligands, namely, $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}{-2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}{-3}$ , respectively. On account of the Lewis acidic $Z\mathbf{r}$ sites, NU-1600 exhibits excellent catalytic performance toward the destruction of nerve agent simulants in the presence of both liquid and solid bases.
The alb net, constructed from 6-c trigonal-prismatic SBUs and 12-c hexagonal-prismatic SBUs, is one of the most intriguing binodal edge-transitive nets.21 The (6,12)- $\cdot\mathbf{c}$ alb net is a highly connected net that could potentially be realized in $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs by rational design and precise control of the geometry of the organic linkers. In this work, the augmented alb (alb-a) net was used to illustrate the topological network. Threefold-disordered $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes have been observed in $\mathrm{PCN-}223^{42}$ and $\mathrm{NU}.904^{44}$ with the overall topology of the $^{4,12\mathrm{-c}}$ shp net, where the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes can be viewed as rare hexagonal-prismatic SBUs. We anticipated that the use of rigid trigonal-prismatic PET-1 ligands would guide the in situ formation of $12\mathrm{-c}\ Z\mathbf{r}_{6}$ nodes as hexagonal-prismatic SBUs, thus resulting in a 3-periodic structure with the alb net.
Indeed, single crystals of NU-1600 (Figure 2) with suitable sizes for SCXRD investigation were obtained after careful finetuning of the synthetic conditionsi.e., $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},$ PET-1, and acidic modulators were dissolved in $N,N$ -diethylformamide (DEF), and the mixture was then kept at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 4 days. SCXRD analysis revealed that NU-1600 crystallizes in space group $P2/m$ $\dot{\langle a=21.632\AA(1).636=12.2815\AA(5).6,c=21.62\dot{7}}$ (1) $\bar{\mathrm{\bf~A}},$ and $\beta=119.975(2)^{\circ}$ at $100\mathrm{K})$ with an ideal formula $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})_{6}(\mathrm{HCOO})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{PET}\mathrm{-}1)_{2},$ without considering the missing-linker defects (Table S1 and Figure S2). Each PET-1 ligand of NU-1600 is coordinated to six $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes, and each threefold-disordered $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ node is coordinated to 12 PET-1 linkers, rendering the expected 6,12-c alb net. The $12\mathrm{-c}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node of NU-1600, which can be viewed as a hexagonal-prismatic SBU, is different from the 12-c $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ node of UiO-66i.e., a cuboctahedron SBU (Figure S3). To the best of our knowledge, the alb net was a missing edge-transitive net for the welldeveloped functional $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs until we uncovered it. NU-1600 can alternatively be further analyzed as an (3,3,12)-c alb-derived ury net if the ligand is deconstructed into two 3-c SBUs.24,43
NU-1600 contains one type of diamond-shaped channels with a dimension of ca. $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ along the $b$ axis and two-dimensional honeycomb-shaped channels in the ac plane (Figure S4). The microcrystalline sample of NU-1600 was synthesized with sizes of $1-2\mu\mathrm{m},$ as revealed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) (Figure S11). The phase purity of the bulk NU-1600 was confirmed by the well-matched simulated and as-synthesized powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns (Figure S5). The permanent porosity of NU-1600 was confirmed by the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm at 77 K with an estimated apparent BET surface area of $2085\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figures 3 and S14). It is interesting to note that the experimental total PV of $0.82~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is higher than the simulated valuei.e., $0.69\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the structure when PET-1 has $100\%$ occupancy inside NU-1600 but agrees well with the simulated PVi.e., $0.83~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ when PET-1 has $70\%$ occupancy (Table S5). In fact, the best crystallographic refinement of the SCXRD data was obtained for NU-1600 when the occupancy level of PET-1 is around $70\%$ , indicating the presence of missing-linker defects.46−48 The pore size distribution based on a density functional theory (DFT) model from the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm revealed one type of main pore centered at $1.2\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3B), which agrees with the size of the diamond-shaped channels. Extra pores ranging from 1.5 to $2.2\mathrm{nm}$ could be associated with the missing-linker defects.
Highly connected edge-transitive nets induce higher degrees of designability with precise choices of building units. They offer more possibilities toward elongated isoreticular structures without catenation. $^{24,26,49-51}$ Solvothermal reactions of longer peripherally extended triptycene ligandsi.e., $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}{-2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{PET}{-3}$ and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ under synthetic conditions similar to those for NU-1600 yielded colorless hexagonal block crystals of NU-1601 and NU-1602, respectively. SCXRD studies revealed that both materials have noncatenated structures crystallizing in space group $P2/m$ (NU-1601: $a=28.130\big(3\big)$ , $b=16.735(2),$ $c=$ 28.174(3) (1) $\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ and $\beta=120.036(4)^{\circ}$ at 275 K; NU-1602: $a=$ 31.678(8), $b=19.591$ (4), $c=31.774(1)\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ and $\beta=119.631(2)^{\circ}$ at 275 K). The phase purities of bulk NU-1601 and NU-1602 were confirmed by the well-matched simulated and assynthesized PXRD patterns (Figures S6 and S7). Topological analysis revealed that NU-1601 and NU-1602 have the same underlying 6,12-c alb topology as NU-1600 with threefolddisordered $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes and extended PET ligands as 12-c hexagonal-prismatic and 6-c trigonal-prismatic SBUs, respectively.
${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of NU-1601 and NU-1602 at $77\mathrm{K}$ after supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation revealed that both structures are mesoporous MOFs with experimental total PVs of 1.75 and $2.36~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}~{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}.$ , respectively, at $P/P_{0}=0.95$ . NU-1601 and NU1602 exhibited ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptakes of 1133 and $1531~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ respectively at 77 K and $P/P_{0}=0.95$ . The pore size distributions of NU-1601 and NU-1602 based on DFT models from the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms revealed main pores centered at 1.4 and $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which agree with the sizes of the diamond channels. Similar to NU-1600, the extra mesoporesi.e., 2.2 and $2.7~\mathrm{nm}$ can be attributed to missing-linker defects. The apparent BET areas of NU-1601 and NU-1602 were estimated to be 3970 and $4500{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}.$ , respectively, after the four BET consistency criteria were satisfied with correlation coefficients higher than 0.995 (Figures S15 and S16 and Table S4).52
$Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs have been widely evaluated for the hydrolysis of dimethyl 4-nitrophenyl phosphonate (DMNP), a nerve agent simulant mimicking the reactivity of organophosphonate-based nerve agents, on account of Lewis acidic sites on the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ nodes.4,53−58 However, systematic studies of isoreticular
Moreover, NU-1600 can efficiently hydrolyze DMNP in the presence of a solid basei.e., linear PEIwith $t_{1/2}\approx3$ min and $\mathrm{\hat{T}O F=0.05~s^{-1}}$ . After washing with water, the NU-1600/PEI composite displays similar reactivity, confirming the heterogeneous nature of the PEI base (Figure S19), which is more practical for protective suit and mask applications.54 NU-1601 and NU-1602 show similar performance as NU-1600 toward the hydrolysis of DMNP, but the hydrolysis is slightly slower $\left(t_{1/2}\approx\$ 3.2 and $2.5\mathrm{min}$ , respectively). These data suggest that the higher degree of missing-linker defects in NU-1600 compared with either NU-1601 or NU-1602, as illustrated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) under air flow (Figures S8−S10 and Table S3), induces a larger number of Lewis acidic $\mathrm{Zr}$ catalytic sites for the hydrolysis to occur, since the particle sizes of these isostructural materials are similar.
The NU-1600/PEI composite enhances the hydrolysis rate of an actual nerve agent, soman (GD), compared with NU-1600. The conversion of GD to the nontoxic pinacolyl methylphosphonic acid (PMPA) as a function of time is shown in Figure 4D, and $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ NMR spectra show the conversion of PMPA (Figures S20−S22). The enhanced reactivity arising from the solid-phase PEI base is clear, with $50\%$ of GD reacting in the presence of NU-1600/PEI sample in under $12\ \mathrm{min}$ , compared with only $20\%$ of GD reacting on baseline NU-1600 after $400~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The rate constants calculated from a first-order kinetic model are 6.2 $\times10^{-4}$ and $5.4\times10^{-2}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for NU-1600 and NU-1600/PEI, respectively. These results demonstrated that the NU-1600/PEI composite can efficiently detoxify the chemical warfare agent.
In conclusion, we have rationally designed and synthesized a series of missing $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs, the NU-160X series $(X=0,1,2)$ , with the edge-transitive 6,12-c alb topology. The highly connected alb net provides a blueprint for programmable noncatenated MOF structures with tunable surface areas and tailorable porosities ranging from microporous to mesoporous. These structurally defective $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs display notable activities toward the efficient destruction of a nerve agent as well as its simulant in the presence of liquid and solid bases. This research illustrates the power of reticular chemistry for the rational design and synthesis of functional highly connected materials and paves the way for correlating unique material properties with desired functionalities.
